# Metabolic Factors and Drug Effects on the ECG

- **Principles** - metabolic abnormalities and pharmacologic agents can result in:
  - Repolarisation abnormalities (altered ST-T-U complex)
  - QRS prolongation
- **Common metabolic and pharmacological factors a/w ECG changes and life-threatening arrhythmias:**
  - <u>Electrolytes</u> - hyperkalaemia, hypokalaemia, hypocalcaemia, hypercalcaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hyper-magnesaemia
  - <u>Pharmacological agents</u> - class IA anti-arrhythmic agents, class III anti-arrhythmics, tri-cyclic antidepressants, phenothiazides, digoxin
  - <u>Other systemic disturbances</u> - systemic hypothermia, sub-arachnoid haemorrhages
- **Hyperkalaemia and associated ECG changes:**
  - <u>Classical progression of ECG changes</u>:
    - Tenting of T waves
    - Widening of QRS complex
    - Flattening of P wave and progressive PR prolongation (a/w **bradyarrhythmias**, and **escape rhythms)**
    - Sine-wave pattern
    - Asystole

    
    ![](../img/ecg_changes_in_hyperk_harrisons.png)
- **Hypokalaemia and associated ECG changes:**
  - <u>Pathophysiology</u> - prolongation of ventricular repolarisation
  - <u>Classical ECG findings</u>:
    - Prominent U waves
    - QT prolongation
- **Hypercalcaemia and hypocalcaemia and associated ECG changes:**
  - <u>Hypocalcaemia</u> - QT prolongation (ST portion)
  - <u>Hypercalcaemia</u> - short QT intervals
- **Systemic hypothermia and ECG changes:**
  - Convex elevation of J point (Osborn wave)
  - Bradycardia

  
  ![](../img/osborn_wave_in_hypothermia.png)
- **Subarachnoid haemorrhages and ECG changes:**
  - Marked QT prolongation
  - Cerebral T waves (deep and wide TWI)

  
  ![](../img/sah_cerebral_t_waves.png)
